WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.480 --> 00:00:06.650
A towering, thin creature stalks the countryside
of the Llanos region in South America carrying

00:00:06.650 --> 00:00:13.960
the bones of his victims in a sack —whistling
all the way.

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:18.150
Vengeful and full of murderous intent, if
you hear his whistle it’s probably already

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:19.779
too late.

00:00:19.779 --> 00:00:25.030
This “Terror of the Plain,” is El Silbón,
or The Whistler, and just wait until you hear

00:00:25.030 --> 00:00:29.939
why this one-time human became the monster
he is now.

00:00:29.939 --> 00:00:34.040
[Monstrum intro.]

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:38.800
If you are unfortunate enough to see El Silbón,
you’ll be looking at an emaciated figure

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:43.800
more than three meters tall, with elongated
limbs and with an old sack slung over his

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:44.800
back.&nbsp;

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:50.610
Also known as El Sin Fin, The Endless, he’s
a malevolent ghost with a very real physical

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:56.220
presence—the result of being cursed by his
grandfather for killing his own father.

00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:01.449
He roams the plains of the Los Llanos region,
a large area of tropical grasslands bordered

00:01:01.449 --> 00:01:06.190
by the Andes in the north and west, and the
Amazon rainforest in the south.&nbsp;

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:10.690
Knowing that El Silbón is so connected to
that landscape, I wanted to learn more about

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:15.380
this South American boogeyman from someone
who had actually been to the land he haunts.&nbsp;

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:21.580
So I contacted Argentinian film director (and
monster enthusiast) Juan Fernández Gebauer,

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:27.140
who worked on Fantasmagorias, a beautifully,
and eerily, illustrated HBO series about the

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:29.240
myths and legends of Latin America.&nbsp;

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:32.663
Thank you so much for taking the time to speak
with me Juan!

00:01:32.663 --> 00:01:34.727
&nbsp;
[Juan: “Hello Doc, it’s a pleasure to

00:01:34.727 --> 00:01:36.537
be on your show, I really like the concept.”]

00:01:36.537 --> 00:01:37.758
&nbsp;
Thank you!

00:01:37.758 --> 00:01:39.192
So what have you heard about the monster?

00:01:39.192 --> 00:01:41.311
I know you spent some time in Columbia.&nbsp;

00:01:41.311 --> 00:01:49.215
El Silbón is a specter from the Los Llanos area. He is the soul in pain of a young man who murdered his father. 

00:01:49.215 --> 00:01:54.824
Cursed by such action, he wanders the plains carrying his father's bones in a sack, 

00:01:54.824 --> 00:02:00.000
throwing gloomy whistles into the air, which is where his name comes from.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.930
There’s a phrase I really like: Each era
vomits the monsters it needs.

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:11.420
Since that first time, I have heard the legend
of the whistler many times more.

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:15.500
Each narrator adapts it a little to their
needs.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:22.569
There are those who say that it was a rude
and spoiled boy who killed and butchered his

00:02:22.569 --> 00:02:29.809
own father, because the man did not manage
to hunt a deer that the boy wanted to eat.&nbsp;

00:02:29.809 --> 00:02:32.970
&nbsp;
The version I was told says that the father

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:37.060
had discovered the boy with a woman he didn't
like.

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:38.060
A prostitute.

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:40.459
Angry, the man murdered the woman.

00:02:40.459 --> 00:02:46.159
The son, who was madly in love, killed his
father in revenge.

00:02:46.159 --> 00:02:50.760
So the reasons why El Silbón is reported
to have killed his father vary—to benefit

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:52.579
the period and the storyteller.

00:02:52.579 --> 00:02:57.480
I’ve also read versions where El Silbón’s
father seduced or assaulted his daughter-in-law,

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.980
triggering his son’s homicidal rage.&nbsp;

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:04.870
And in other stories, the young man’s father
murdered his wife not because she was a prostitute,

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:08.129
but because she was rumored to be promiscuous.

00:03:08.129 --> 00:03:13.340
Regardless of why the young man killed his
father, all of the stories end one way.

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:19.310
[Juan: Everyone agrees that after seeing the
killing, the boy's grandfather cursed him,

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:27.159
tortured him with a whip and washed his wounds
with Aguardiente and then released him to

00:03:27.159 --> 00:03:29.279
some hungry and rabid dogs.&nbsp;

00:03:29.279 --> 00:03:33.380
&nbsp;
And since then the Whistler has been roaming

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:41.909
the roads, carrying his father's remains in
a sack and murdering drunks, partiers, womanizers.

00:03:41.909 --> 00:03:46.499
And whistling his melody.]

00:03:46.499 --> 00:03:52.519
El Silbón doesn’t just wander around aimlessly—he
hunts for womanizers and cheaters.

00:03:52.519 --> 00:03:56.529
Perhaps because of the role his murderous
father and the woman he loved played in his

00:03:56.529 --> 00:03:57.529
fate.&nbsp;

00:03:57.529 --> 00:04:02.969
And he also looks for drunks—which might
be because of the alcohol his grandfather

00:04:02.969 --> 00:04:05.969
drenched his wounds with before his death.

00:04:05.969 --> 00:04:11.129
But no one is safe—the occasional innocent
victim may fall into his path.

00:04:11.129 --> 00:04:15.980
You won't always hear the rattling of his
victims’ bones from his sack as he approaches—but

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:20.810
you’ll always hear the rise and fall of
his haunting whistle.

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:29.610
In this Venezuelan story, we learn just how
cruel El Silbón can be.

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:31.700
One night a rancher was hosting a party.

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:36.260
A man planned on going, but his friends warned
him it was dangerous to walk through the plains

00:04:36.260 --> 00:04:39.260
alone on a dark night like this one.

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:44.640
But the man insisted he could protect himself
and set off for the celebration on his horse.&nbsp;

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:49.370
The horse ran fast, but upon crossing a stream
it stopped abruptly.

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:54.340
The man heard a whistled scale in the distance
as he tried to calm his horse...the whistling

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:56.390
was getting closer.

00:04:56.390 --> 00:04:58.880
The horse spooked and threw the man off.

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:02.550
Suddenly the whistling faded as if the whistler
were going away.

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:08.880
But when the man turned, he saw a figure with
painfully thin, elongated limbs so tall he

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:10.850
could not see its face.

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:15.620
The figure hit the man with a stick, and even
with his machete, the man could not reach

00:05:15.620 --> 00:05:17.820
the creature to defend himself.

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:21.560
The monster beat the man to the ground and
walked away laughing.&nbsp;

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:26.040
The man managed to find his horse and endured
a painful ride to the party.

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:31.460
Those celebrating asked what had happened
and when he told them—they all crossed themselves.

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:34.500
The owner of the ranch told him he had come
upon El Silbón.

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:39.150
And he was a lucky one—most of El Silbón’s
victims do not survive.&nbsp;

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:44.290
El Silbón clearly likes to toy with his prey.

00:05:44.290 --> 00:05:49.530
There’s that unusual, and creepy, characteristic
of his whistle: when you hear it close by,

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:55.140
that’s a sign you are actually safe; the
further away the whistle sounds, the closer

00:05:55.140 --> 00:05:56.961
to you El Silbón really is.&nbsp;

00:05:56.961 --> 00:06:02.910
He is particularly violent with drunks—sucking
out the alcohol from a hole in their stomachs—before

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:06.640
removing their bones from the same opening
he drank from.

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:12.120
And taking the bones is the worst part: without
them his victims cannot rest peacefully in

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:16.170
death, as they have become part of El Silbón’s
doomed existence.

00:06:16.170 --> 00:06:20.110
If you hear El Silbón outside your door at
night and fail to do anything to drive him

00:06:20.110 --> 00:06:23.490
away, or if you don’t stop to listen to
his whistling

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:25.710
—a member of your household will die.

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:29.200
A surefire way to drive away his lost soul
is

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:32.370
with a dog—since he hates the animals that
tore him apart.

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:36.720
This is such a unique, and eerie monster.

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:38.930
What possibly could have inspired it?

00:06:38.930 --> 00:06:45.000
[Juan: From what I was able to find out from
locals, the legend of El Silbón is based

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:51.570
on a person named Joaquín Flores, who probably
committed a crime similar to this one around

00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:57.740
1850 , and as in other times events of this
nature were rare, the men from the plains

00:06:57.740 --> 00:07:05.750
took moral advantage of the murder and echoed
the story so that similar events would not

00:07:05.750 --> 00:07:07.050
be repeated.]

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:12.580
Originally, the land of the Llanos was populated
by nomadic indigenous groups like the Guahibo.

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:17.370
By the 19th century when tales of El Silbón
took root, many of the land’s occupants

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:18.690
were cattle ranchers.&nbsp;

00:07:18.690 --> 00:07:24.260
Livestock was introduced to the Llanos region
by the Spanish colonizers in 1548, and the

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:31.070
cattle population rose to an estimated 4.5
million by the beginning of the 19th Century.

00:07:31.070 --> 00:07:36.090
This likely explains El Silbón’s distinctive
hat; the style commonly worn by ranchers of

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:37.090
the region.

00:07:37.090 --> 00:07:41.480
As the cattle population boomed, tensions
rose against the Spanish occupiers in the

00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:43.530
Llanos region and surrounding areas.

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:48.300
The Llaneros, a name that appears during this
century to refer to all people who worked

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:51.200
the ranches, joined the revolutionary forces

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:54.070
of Simón Bolívar in 1818.

00:07:54.070 --> 00:07:58.460
As skilled horsemen and fighters, they helped
deal the crucial blow to the Spanish crown

00:07:58.460 --> 00:08:00.320
in the battle of Boyacá.

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:04.000
Following the deaths in the Venezuelan War
of Independence and the continued wartime

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:10.830
devastation to crops and livestock, by 1823
only 256,000 cattle remained.&nbsp;

00:08:10.830 --> 00:08:15.300
So, monumental changes occurred when the Spanish
introduced cattle to the Llanos and when the

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:19.600
ranchers of those cattle contributed to the
independence of the area from Spanish rule.

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:24.610
The impact of these events is reflected in
the appearance of the region’s most famous

00:08:24.610 --> 00:08:26.100
monster, El Silbón.

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:28.810
And the legend and impact continues.

00:08:28.810 --> 00:08:31.470
Since 1974, an annual Venezuelan music [Festival
Internacional de Música Llanera "El Silbón"]

00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:35.120
festival celebrates El Silbon and “Llanero
folklore.”

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:37.490
He has a statue in a Venezuelan theme park,

00:08:37.490 --> 00:08:39.209
his own horror movies, and was even

00:08:39.209 --> 00:08:44.070
referenced in a 2003 speech by former Venezuelan
President Hugo Chávez.&nbsp;

00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:48.949
El Silbón has left his impact on popular
culture, but that doesn’t mean this monster

00:08:48.949 --> 00:08:50.750
has lost his influence in the Llanos.&nbsp;

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:57.610
[Juan: Even the young people who sometimes
laugh incredulously and make jokes about his

00:08:57.610 --> 00:09:03.490
appearances, when asked seriously if they
fear him, say that yes, it is feasible to

00:09:03.490 --> 00:09:09.810
hear his piercing whistle late at night in
the streets adjacent to the center of town.

00:09:09.810 --> 00:09:11.630
&nbsp;
I believe that telling the legends and traditions

00:09:11.630 --> 00:09:14.089
of a culture helps to preserve an identity.

00:09:14.089 --> 00:09:19.970
Understanding what scares us, helps us to
understand what is hidden, what is in the

00:09:19.970 --> 00:09:20.970
shadows.]

00:09:20.970 --> 00:09:25.810
Today the myth of El Silbón is an active
part of the folklore of the Llanos, continuing

00:09:25.810 --> 00:09:28.330
a tradition hundreds of years old.&nbsp;

